; hd44780_4bit.loom
; Loom drives an HD44780U character LCD controller (Hitachi, the LCD-II of
; most 16x2 modules) over its 4-bit parallel bus: the power-on
; initialization by instruction, then instructions, data bytes and "print
; this string at this DDRAM address" from the host, with the busy flag read
; back over the same data pins after every byte.
;
; Written on 2026-11-05, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol Loom was not designed for (D-035). The HD44780 bus is not serial
; and is in no design document: a register select (RS), a direction (R/W)
; and an enable strobe (E) frame a 4-bit data nibble, with set-up and hold
; windows around E, waits of milliseconds during the power-on sequence, and
; a busy-flag read that turns the data pins around. The program uses:
;   - the OUT and IN pin groups (OUTGRP, INGRP), built to change or read
;     several pins in one slot (spi_master.loom writes {CS_n, SCK, MOSI}
;     with one OUT), to put a nibble on D4..D7 in one slot and to read one
;     back in one slot: a 4-bit parallel port;
;   - OEP, the per-pin output enable of the BIDIR pins, to hand D4..D7 to
;     the LCD for the busy-flag read and take them back (the bus
;     turnaround swd_master.loom does on SWDIO, four pins wide);
;   - the deadline schedule (SETD, WAITD) on a 1 us tick for the E strobe,
;     and DLY, built for timeouts, for the long waits of the datasheet.
; What the firmware does that no hardware does: the E strobe and its
; set-up and hold, the nibble order, the initialization flowchart, the
; busy-flag poll (two reads per poll, Figure 9), the execution-time waits
; of the timed path, the DDRAM addressing of a 2-line display and the
; host's string transfers. Weaker than a dedicated peripheral: the bus runs
; at one tick per E phase, 2 us a nibble against the 1 us t_cycE the
; datasheet allows, which costs nothing next to the 37 us an instruction
; takes inside the LCD; and while the LCD is busy the thread polls.
;
; Source: the HD44780U datasheet, ADE-207-272(Z), '99.9, Rev. 0.0 (printed
; page numbers). The I/O is 3.3 V, so the program keeps to the VCC = 2.7 to
; 4.5 V column (p.49): t_cycE >= 1000 ns, PW_EH >= 450 ns, t_Er/t_Ef <= 25
; ns, t_AS >= 60 ns, t_AH >= 20 ns, t_DSW >= 195 ns, t_H >= 10 ns, t_DDR
; <= 360 ns, t_DHR >= 5 ns (Figures 25 and 26, p.58). A 5 V module (p.52's
; column) would drive 5 V into Loom's pins during a read: use a 3.3 V
; module, or the timed path with R/W tied low.
;
; Pins: D4 = BIDIR0, D5 = BIDIR1, D6 = BIDIR2, D7 = BIDIR3 (consecutive:
; one OUT group and one IN group), RS = OUT0, R/W = OUT1, E = OUT2. D0..D3
; of the module are not connected (p.22: "Bus lines DB0 to DB3 are
; disabled" in 4-bit mode). Seven pins: the pin budget allows R/W, so the
; busy flag is read; the timed path is there for boards that tie R/W low.
;
; Host words (FIFO space, thread 0). INQ, one operation per word, bits
; 15:12 the operation:
;   0x00nm  initialize (Figure 24, p.46). m = bit 0: timed path (R/W tied
;           low: wait the execution times instead of reading the busy flag);
;           bit 1: the supply has been up for more than 40 ms already (a
;           re-initialization), skip the power-on wait. n = bits 3:2: N and
;           F of the function set (bit 3 two lines, bit 2 the 5x10 font),
;           so 0x0008 is a 2-line display, busy flag, from power-on. Any
;           other operation before the first initialize is refused.
;   0x1 0ii instruction ii (bits 7:0), RS = 0 (Table 6, p.24).
;   0x2 0dd data byte dd, RS = 1: a character code into DDRAM (or CGRAM).
;   0x3 print: bits 11:7 n (0..31 characters), bits 6:0 the DDRAM address
;           (p.11, Figure 4: row 0 from 0x00, row 1 from 0x40, so row r,
;           column c is 0x40 * r + c), followed in INQ by ceil(n / 2) data
;           words, character 2k in bits 7:0 and 2k+1 in bits 15:8 of word
;           k. Sends Set DDRAM address (0x80 + address), then the n data
;           bytes; the address counter increments in the entry mode the
;           initialization set (I/D = 1).
;   0x4000  read the busy flag and address counter (needs R/W).
; OUTQ, one result word per operation: bits 15:12 the operation, and for
; operation 4 bits 6:0 the address counter (bit 7 the busy flag, 0 by then).
; Bit 15 set: an error, bits 11:8 say which. 0x1: the busy flag stayed set
; for BFMAX polls (no LCD, or a dead one), the operation abandoned (data
; words still in INQ dropped); 0xF: not an operation, or not now (operation
; 4 on the timed path, anything before the first initialize).
;
; Timing, with a 1 us tick (TICK_INT = US, set here; 50 at 50 MHz):
;   - a nibble: RS and R/W set, then D4..D7, then E high for one tick and
;     low again; the next nibble re-anchors, so E is low for more than one
;     tick and rises at most every two. Each edge lands 0 to 3 clocks
;     after its tick (the slot grid, SEMANTICS 2), so t_cycE is two ticks
;     less up to 3 clocks (1.92 us measured at 25 MHz, 2.0 at 50) and
;     PW_EH one tick less up to 3 clocks (0.96 us); t_AS and t_DSW are
;     more than a tick (the data is set more than a tick before E rises
;     and held until after it falls); t_AH and t_H one slot (4 clocks) or
;     more: nothing changes in the slot that drops E (0.88 and 0.40 us
;     measured at 50 MHz);
;   - a busy-flag read: D4..D7 released, RS = 0, R/W = 1, more than a tick,
;     then two E pulses of one tick, D4..D7 sampled at the end of each
;     (t_DDR <= 360 ns after the rise), BF = D7 of the first; then more than
;     a tick with E low before R/W falls and D4..D7 are driven again (the
;     datasheet gives t_DHR >= 5 ns but no maximum: a tick is the margin);
;   - the timed path: 61 us after a byte, 2.2 ms after Clear display or
;     Return home, from Table 6 (p.24, 37 us and 1.52 ms at fOSC = 270 kHz)
;     scaled to the slowest oscillator of p.49 (190 kHz): 37 * 270 / 190 =
;     52.6 us, plus t_ADD = 1.5 / fOSC = 7.9 us (p.25, Figure 10), and 1.52
;     ms * 270 / 190 = 2.16 ms. Table 6 prints no time for Clear display;
;     the program gives it Return home's (QUESTIONS.md);
;   - initialization: more than 40 ms (the 2.7 V line of Figure 24: the
;     program cannot see the supply rise, so it waits from the operation),
;     0x3, more than 4.1 ms, 0x3, more than 100 us, 0x3, then 0x2 (one
;     nibble each, the LCD still in 8-bit mode: "BF cannot be checked
;     before this instruction"), each of the last two followed by the
;     timed 61 us; then function set 0x2n (two nibbles), display off 0x08,
;     clear 0x01 and entry mode 0x06 (increment, no shift), each with the
;     busy flag (or the timed waits). Display on is the host's: 0x100C.
; The schedule is proved for ticks of 12 clocks or more (US below 12 fails
; the checker), so the program runs from a 12 MHz clock up with US set to
; the clock in MHz; the tests run the RTL with US 25, a 25 MHz clock.
;
; Memory: 181 words. The operations and the initialization in thread 0's
; quarter (0x000..0x06D), the byte, nibble and busy-flag subroutines in
; thread 3's (0x180..0x1C6), as tm1637_controller.loom does: threads 1 and
; 2 keep their quarters, which bridge_uart_lcd.loom's receiver uses.
;
; Registers: r0 the byte being sent, r1 counts (polls, wait loops), r2
; scratch and the nibble, r3 the busy flag and AC read, r4 the mode (bit 0
; timed, bit 15 initialized), r5 RS of the byte (1 data), r6 characters
; still to print, r7 the operation word.
;
; Errors: r3 bit 15 is sticky within an operation. Once a byte's busy-flag
; poll times out, the operation's later bytes are not sent, but their data
; words are still taken from INQ, so the next word read is an operation.

        .imem   512                     ; the macro build: quarters of 128 words
        .thread 0
        .pins   D4 = BIDIR0, D5 = BIDIR1, D6 = BIDIR2, D7 = BIDIR3
        .pins   RS = OUT0, RW = OUT1, E = OUT2
        .equ    US = 50                 ; clocks per microsecond at 50 MHz
        .equ    GRP = pin(D4) | (4 << 5)        ; D4..D7: four pins from D4
        .equ    BFMAX = 1250            ; polls, over 4 us each: 5 ms > 2.16 ms
        .equ    T_BYTE = 62             ; DLY: more than 61 us
        .equ    T_LONG = 200            ; DLY, 11 times: more than 2.18 ms
        .equ    N_LONG = 11
        .equ    OP_INIT = 0
        .equ    OP_INST = 1
        .equ    OP_DATA = 2
        .equ    OP_PRINT = 3
        .equ    OP_READ = 4

start:  .csr    OUTGRP, GRP             ; OUT r writes D4..D7 from r[3:0]
        .csr    INGRP, GRP              ; IN r reads them into r[3:0]
        .csr    TICK_INT, US            ; one tick = 1 us; also the checker's .tick
        LDI     r7, 0
        CSRW    TICK_FRAC, r7
        SETP    E, 0                    ; E low: the LCD ignores the bus
        SETP    RS, 0
        SETP    RW, 0                   ; write: the LCD never drives D4..D7
        OUT     r7                      ; D4..D7 low,
        CALL    drive                   ; and driven
        LDI     r4, 0                   ; not initialized

cmd:    POP     r7                      ; next operation (blocks while INQ is empty)
        LDI     r3, 0                   ; no error yet
        MOV     r2, r7
        SHRI    r2, 12                  ; the operation
        CMPI    r2, OP_INIT
        BZ      init
        TEST    r4, r4
        BZ      bad                     ; nothing before the first initialize
        CMPI    r2, OP_INST
        BZ      inst
        CMPI    r2, OP_DATA
        BZ      data
        CMPI    r2, OP_PRINT
        BZ      print
        CMPI    r2, OP_READ
        BNZ     bad
        MOV     r2, r4                  ; read BF and AC: R/W must be wired
        ANDI    r2, 1
        BNZ     bad
        CALL    bfpoll                  ; r3 = AC, or the error
        JMP     done

inst:   MOV     r0, r7
        CALL    wins
        JMP     ok
data:   MOV     r0, r7
        CALL    wdat
        JMP     ok

; Print: Set DDRAM address (0x80 + bits 6:0), then n characters, two a word.
print:  MOV     r6, r7
        SHRI    r6, 7
        ANDI    r6, 31                  ; n
        MOV     r0, r7
        SHLI    r0, 9
        SHRI    r0, 9                   ; the address
        LDI     r2, 0x80
        OR      r0, r0, r2
        CALL    wins
pr_word: TEST   r6, r6
        BZ      ok                      ; every character sent
        POP     r0                      ; two characters; E waits low meanwhile
        CALL    wdat                    ; character 2k (wbyte keeps r0)
        DEC     r6
        BZ      ok                      ; n odd: the high byte unused
        SWAP    r0, r0
        CALL    wdat                    ; character 2k + 1
        DEC     r6
        JMP     pr_word

bad:    LDI     r3, 0x8F                ; not an operation, or not now
        SWAP    r3, r3                  ; 0x8F00
        JMP     done
ok:     MOV     r2, r3
        SHLI    r2, 1                   ; C = bit 15: an error to report?
        BC      done
        LDI     r3, 0                   ; no: the AC a poll left is not a result
done:   MOV     r2, r7                  ; result = operation << 12 | r3
        SHRI    r2, 12
        SHLI    r2, 12
        OR      r3, r3, r2
        PUSH    r3                      ; blocks while OUTQ is full: E stays low
        JMP     cmd

; Initializing by instruction, 4-bit interface (Figure 24, p.46).
init:   MOV     r4, r7
        ANDI    r4, 1                   ; the timed path?
        SETP    RS, 0
        MOV     r2, r7
        ANDI    r2, 2
        BNZ     in_warm                 ; the supply is already up
        LDI     r1, 165
in_pwr: DLY     250                     ; 165 * 250 us: more than 41 ms
        DJNZ    r1, in_pwr
in_warm: LDI    r2, 3
        CALL    wnib                    ; function set, 8 bits (one nibble)
        LDI     r1, 20
in_41:  DLY     210                     ; 20 * 210 us: more than 4.18 ms
        DJNZ    r1, in_41
        LDI     r2, 3
        CALL    wnib                    ; function set, 8 bits
        DLY     102                     ; more than 101 us
        LDI     r2, 3
        CALL    wnib                    ; function set, 8 bits
        DLY     T_BYTE                  ; its execution time (BF not yet usable)
        LDI     r2, 2
        CALL    wnib                    ; function set: 4 bits from now on
        DLY     T_BYTE
        LDI     r2, 0x80
        SWAP    r2, r2
        OR      r4, r4, r2              ; bit 15: initialized
        MOV     r0, r7
        ANDI    r0, 0x0C                ; N and F
        ORI     r0, 0x20
        CALL    wins                    ; function set 0x2n, two nibbles
        LDI     r0, 0x08
        CALL    wins                    ; display off
        LDI     r0, 0x01
        CALL    wins                    ; display clear
        LDI     r0, 0x06
        CALL    wins                    ; entry mode: increment, no shift
        JMP     ok

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The spill: from here on the program sits in thread 3's quarter, 0x180 on
; (see "Memory" above). Thread 3 must not be started with this image.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
        .org    0x180

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; wins, wdat: send the byte r0[7:0] as an instruction (RS = 0) or as data
; (RS = 1), high nibble first (p.22), then wait until the LCD is ready:
; the busy-flag poll, or the timed wait. Keeps r0. Skips everything if an
; earlier byte of the operation failed (r3 bit 15).
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
wins:   SETP    RS, 0
        LDI     r5, 0
        JMP     wbyte
wdat:   SETP    RS, 1
        LDI     r5, 1
wbyte:  MOV     r2, r3
        SHLI    r2, 1
        BC      wb_ret                  ; the operation has failed already
        MOV     r2, r0
        SHRI    r2, 4
        CALL    wnib                    ; D7..D4 of the byte
        MOV     r2, r0
        CALL    wnib                    ; D3..D0 (OUT takes bits 3:0)
        MOV     r2, r4
        ANDI    r2, 1
        BZ      bfpoll                  ; R/W wired: read the busy flag
        TEST    r5, r5
        BNZ     wb_short                ; data: 37 us
        MOV     r2, r0
        SHLI    r2, 8
        SHRI    r2, 10                  ; byte >> 2 (the byte alone)
        BNZ     wb_short                ; not Clear display or Return home
        LDI     r1, N_LONG
wb_long: DLY    T_LONG
        DJNZ    r1, wb_long
wb_ret: RET
wb_short: DLY   T_BYTE
        RET

; wnib: one nibble, r2[3:0] on D4..D7, RS and R/W already set. E rises
; more than a tick after the data is set, stays high one tick, and falls;
; nothing else changes in that slot.
wnib:   SETD    1
        OUT     r2
        WAITD   1                       ; more than a tick: t_AS, t_DSW
        SETP    E, 1
        WAITD   1                       ; PW_EH
        SETP    E, 0                    ; the LCD takes D4..D7 here
        RET

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; bfpoll: read the busy flag and the address counter (RS = 0, R/W = 1, two
; nibbles: Figure 9, p.22) until BF = 0, at most BFMAX times. Returns r3 =
; AC (bits 6:0), or 0x8100 if the flag never cleared. D4..D7 are driven
; again on return.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
bfpoll: CALL    release                 ; D4..D7 to the LCD
        SETP    RS, 0
        SETP    RW, 1                   ; read: the LCD drives while E is high
        MOV16   r1, BFMAX
bf_lp:  SETD    1
        WAITD   1                       ; more than a tick since R/W or E fell
        SETP    E, 1
        WAITD   1                       ; t_DDR <= 360 ns: valid by now
        IN      r3                      ; BF, AC6, AC5, AC4
        SETP    E, 0
        WAITD   1
        SETP    E, 1
        WAITD   1
        IN      r2                      ; AC3..AC0
        SETP    E, 0
        SHLI    r3, 4
        OR      r3, r3, r2              ; BF and AC in bits 7:0
        MOV     r2, r3
        SHRI    r2, 7                   ; BF
        BZ      bf_end                  ; ready
        DJNZ    r1, bf_lp
        LDI     r3, 0x81                ; never ready: report 0x1
        SWAP    r3, r3                  ; 0x8100
bf_end: DLY     2                       ; E low more than a tick (t_DHR)
        SETP    RW, 0                   ; then back to write,
                                        ; and fall through to drive D4..D7

; drive, release: the output enables of D4..D7, one pin at a time (OEP
; touches only its pin, so another thread's BIDIR pins keep theirs).
drive:  OEP     D4, 1
        OEP     D5, 1
        OEP     D6, 1
        OEP     D7, 1
        RET
release: OEP    D4, 0
        OEP     D5, 0
        OEP     D6, 0
        OEP     D7, 0
        RET
